.TH "HED-KEYMAP" "1" "2026-08-11" "hed v1.19.0" "hed manual"
.SH NAME
hed\-keymap \- Runtime keymap swap.
Without this plugin, you\[cq]re locked to whichever keymap plugin was
loaded with enabled=1 at startup.
With it, you can flip between vim, emacs, and vscode bindings on the
fly.
.PP
Runtime keymap swap.
Without this plugin, you\[cq]re locked to whichever keymap plugin was
loaded with \f[CR]enabled=1\f[R] at startup.
With it, you can flip between vim, emacs, and vscode bindings on the
fly.
.SH Commands
.IP
.EX
:keymap                 # print the current keymap name
:keymap vim             # switch to vim bindings
:keymap emacs           # switch to emacs bindings
:keymap vscode          # switch to vscode bindings
:keymap\-toggle          # cycle: vim → emacs → vscode → vim
.EE
.SH How it works
The three keymap plugins (\f[CR]vim_keybinds\f[R],
\f[CR]emacs_keybinds\f[R], \f[CR]vscode_keybinds\f[R]) are typically
loaded with \f[CR]plugin_load(..., 0)\f[R] \[em] registered but not
enabled.
Switching keymaps clears the existing keybind table and runs the target
keymap\[cq]s \f[CR]init()\f[R], which re\-registers its bindings.
.PP
Keybind dispatch is last\-write\-wins on
\f[CR](mode, sequence, filetype)\f[R], so personal overrides in
\f[CR]src/config.c\f[R] (registered after the keymap\[cq]s
\f[CR]init()\f[R]) survive a swap because they get re\-applied each
time.
Cross\-keymap state like macros and registers persists.
.SH Notes
Modeless behavior switches with the keymap: emacs and vscode set
modeless (always\-INSERT), vim clears it (mode\-aware).
If you don\[cq]t want that, edit \f[CR]src/config.c\f[R] to call
\f[CR]ed_set_modeless(...)\f[R] after your \f[CR]:keymap\f[R] calls.
.PP
\f[CR]<space>tk\f[R] is the default leader binding for
\f[CR]:keymap\-toggle\f[R].
